# R3 — Observable boundaries

Every method on a cross-package boundary interface (see R2) **must** be
wrapped in the `@boundary` decorator (or its TS equivalent) from
`packages/tracing/`. This emits a structured event the Pipeline Trace Viewer
(inspector page 07) reads to render the full request lifecycle.

No hidden state mutations across packages. If it crosses a boundary,
it shows up in the trace.

> Status (2026-06-01): `packages/tracing/` is created in Task 0.4. Until then,
> mark boundary methods with a `// TODO(R3): wrap in @boundary` comment so
> they're easy to find.

## ❌ BAD

```py
# packages/memory-graph/src/graphiti_store.py
class GraphitiStore:
    async def write(self, fact: Fact) -> None:
        await self._client.add_fact(fact)  # silent — no trace
```

## ✅ GOOD

```py
from yellow_ue.tracing import boundary

class GraphitiStore:
    @boundary(name="memory-graph.write")
    async def write(self, fact: Fact) -> None:
        await self._client.add_fact(fact)
```

## What `@boundary` emits

- `name` — stable identifier (`<package>.<method>`)
- `inputs` — args (PII-redacted by allow-list)
- `output` or `error`
- `start_ts`, `end_ts`, `trace_id`, `span_id`

These flow into the structured log stream the Pipeline Trace Viewer consumes.
